# SBE Ingestion Pipeline

This folder contains `ingest.py` — the script that processes Illinois State
Board of Elections receipts files and merges real campaign-finance data into
`council-data.json`.

## How it fits with the rest of the pipeline

```
                    SBE bulk data (manual download)
                              │
                              ▼
   Editor places file in raw/receipts/ and runs ingest.py
                              │
                              ▼
                    council-data.json (committed to repo)
                              │
                              │ (nightly cron)
                              ▼
   sync_overrides.py + sync_votes.py apply Sheet edits
                              │
                              ▼
                    council-data.json (final)
                              │
                              ▼
                    Squarespace embed
```

Ingestion is a **manual quarterly step**, not automated. Filings come out on
predictable cadence (Apr 15, Jul 15, Oct 15, Jan 15), so a few minutes of
editor attention after each deadline is enough.

## What ingestion does

For each receipts file:
1. **Validates schema** — fails loudly if SBE has changed their file format
2. **Parses contributions** — handles all 5 D2Part types (Individual,
   Transfer In, In-kind, Loan Received, Other)
3. **Auto-classifies donors** by name pattern (CTU → labor-teachers, etc.)
4. **Detects self-funding** — candidate giving to their own committee
5. **Aggregates small-dollar** — under-$150 contributions roll up into one
   pseudo-donor per committee per cycle
6. **Merges into council-data.json** — preserves editor overrides from the
   Sheet (industries, flags, notes, cash-on-hand)

## One-time setup

### Download a sample to confirm the format works for you

The first time you run this, do a single committee end-to-end before
attempting multiple. Pick a known committee — La Spata (34616) is a good
test case.

1. Go to https://www.elections.il.gov
2. Campaign Disclosure → Search Committees → search for the committee
3. On the committee's page, find the **Receipts** export
4. Download as **CSV** (the `Receipts.csv` format)
5. Save the file to `raw/receipts/Receipts-ward1-laspata.csv` (the filename
   is arbitrary — the script reads `CommitteeID` from inside the file)

### Build the ward map

`ward-map.json` translates SBE committee IDs to ward numbers. As you ingest
each ward, add to this file. The included file has La Spata's mapping as a
starting point:

```json
{
  "34616": 1
}
```

Add new entries as you ingest new committees. You can also let the script
prompt for the ward number interactively if you skip the ward-map argument.

### Install dependencies

```bash
pip install --user  # nothing needed — pure stdlib
```

The script uses only Python 3.8+ standard library. No pip install required.

## Usage

```bash
# Process all files in raw/receipts/
python ingestion/ingest.py --data-file council-data.json --ward-map ingestion/ward-map.json

# Single file
python ingestion/ingest.py --file raw/receipts/Receipts-ward1.csv --ward-map ingestion/ward-map.json --data-file council-data.json

# Dry run (report changes, don't write)
python ingestion/ingest.py --file raw/receipts/Receipts-ward1.csv --ward-map ingestion/ward-map.json --data-file council-data.json --dry-run
```

After ingestion succeeds:
1. **Commit the updated council-data.json** to the repo
2. Push to GitHub
3. The live site reflects the new data within a few minutes
4. Any Sheet edits (flags, industry overrides) get re-applied by the
   nightly sync, so your editorial work survives

## Quarterly workflow (after first setup)

After each filing deadline (Apr 15, Jul 15, Oct 15, Jan 15):

1. **Download receipts files** for each committee you're tracking. SBE makes
   filings available within hours of submission.
2. **Place them in `raw/receipts/`**. Filenames don't matter — the script
   reads the committee ID from inside each file.
3. **Run ingestion:**
   ```bash
   python ingestion/ingest.py --data-file council-data.json --ward-map ingestion/ward-map.json
   ```
4. **Review the output** — the script reports row counts, total amounts,
   classification breakdowns. Check for warnings.
5. **Commit and push.**

For 50 wards × 4 quarters = 200 downloads/year. Realistically, you'd start
with the most-watched wards (probably the 8 we already have demo data for)
and expand from there. Even 8 wards × 4 quarters = 32 downloads/year is
manageable.

## What's auto-classified vs. what needs editor review

The script's classification rules cover the obvious patterns:

| Pattern in donor name | Classified as |
|---|---|
| CTU, Chicago Teachers Union, federation of teachers | labor-teachers |
| SEIU, hospital workers, nurses | labor-service |
| AFSCME, public employees union | labor-public |
| Carpenters, ironworkers, operating engineers, etc. | labor-trades |
| Union, Local N, Council N, brotherhood (generic) | labor-trades |
| United Working Families, UWF, IPO, grassroots | progressive-pol |
| Real estate, realty, developers, properties, BOMA | real-estate |
| Restaurant, cafe, brewery, Illinois Restaurant | restaurant |
| Bank, JPMorgan, Chase, credit union | finance |
| FOP, fraternal order of police | police-fop |
| Charter schools, KIPP, Noble Network | charter-schools |
| ComEd, Peoples Gas, Chevron, BP | fossil-fuels |
| Cannabis, dispensary, Cresco | cannabis |
| Hospital, medical center, pharmaceutical | healthcare |
| "PAC" (catch-all) | establishment-pol |
| Names with comma (Last, First format) | individual |

Anything that doesn't match these rules → **unclassified**. Editors review
these in the Google Sheet's Donor Overrides tab and set `primary_industry`.

The first run will surface a lot of unclassifieds (companies the rules don't
recognize). Expect ~20-30% of organizational donors to need editor review
on first pass. After classification stabilizes, new filings should be
mostly auto-classified.

## When SBE changes the file format

This is the failure mode to watch for. The script has three defenses:

1. **Schema validation.** Required columns (`CommitteeID`, `CommitteeName`,
   `ContributedBy`, `RcvdDate`, `Amount`, `D2Part`) are checked at the
   start. If any are missing, the script fails with a clear message:
   ```
   Receipts.csv: missing required columns: {'D2Part'}.
     Got: ['CommitteeID', 'CommitteeName', ...]
     This usually means SBE changed their export format.
   ```

2. **Empty file detection.** If the file has no data rows, the script
   fails before touching anything.

3. **Sanity logs.** The script reports parsed/skipped row counts and total
   amount. If these look wrong (e.g., $0 total or way fewer rows than
   expected), don't commit the result.

If SBE adds new columns (forward-compatible), the script silently ignores
them. If they rename columns (breaking change), update `REQUIRED_COLUMNS`
in `ingest.py` and the row-parsing logic accordingly.

## What ingestion does NOT do (and why)

- **Doesn't fetch from SBE directly.** No public API exists for SBE bulk
  data; downloads require manual interaction with the SBE website. We
  could add a scraper, but it would be brittle. Manual download takes 30
  seconds per committee and is more robust.

- **Doesn't calculate cash-on-hand.** That field requires expenditures
  data (which we're not ingesting) plus prior balance. Editor enters it
  manually in the Sheet, where it's preserved across ingestion runs.

- **Doesn't fix name variants.** "United Working Families" and "United
  Working Families PAC" become separate donor records because they
  produce different slugs. Editor can merge via the Sheet (note in the
  override row) or just accept the small duplication.

- **Doesn't run on a schedule.** Filings happen quarterly; running nightly
  would waste resources and download the same data 90 times. Manual
  quarterly runs after each deadline is the right cadence.

## Cleanup: removing the original DEMO data

When you finish ingesting Ward 1 (or any ward), the script automatically
replaces that ward's demo contributions with real data. But cross-alder
demo data (UWF donating to Wards 25, 33, etc.) stays until you ingest
those wards.

Until you've ingested all 50 wards, the donor leaderboard will mix real
and demo data. If you want a clean slate immediately (only real data shown),
manually delete the remaining DEMO committee records from `council-data.json`
or add a future `--wipe-demo` flag to ingest.py. For now, the mixed state
is informative — it shows what the leaderboard will look like once full
ingestion happens.

## Common issues

**"missing required columns: {'D2Part'}"**
SBE changed their export format. Check the column header row of your
downloaded file and update `REQUIRED_COLUMNS` in ingest.py.

**"file is empty (no data rows)"**
The committee has no receipts in the filing period you downloaded, OR
the file didn't download correctly. Re-download and check the file size.

**Auto-classification surprises**
Look at the donor in `council-data.json`. The `industries` array shows
what was classified. To override, add a row to the Sheet's Donor Overrides
tab with `primary_industry` set to the correct value. The override
persists across future ingestions.

**Duplicate donors with similar names**
"Chicago Teachers Union" and "Chicago Teachers Union PAC" produce
different slugs. The current best practice: pick one as canonical (usually
the PAC version, since that's what SBE uses), add a note to the other
saying "see [canonical-slug]", and let editors gradually merge via the
Sheet. Future enhancement: add an `aliases` column to the override sheet.

**SBE bulk download is huge (over 10MB)**
For some committees, the file may exceed GitHub's commit size warnings.
Compress with gzip before committing, or use git-lfs. The script reads
.csv and .txt; if needed, decompress before running ingest.
